window.WIKI_SEARCH = [{"id":"introduction","title":"Introduction","text":"Introduction A practical guide to modding Ballistic.exe with UE4SS and Lua. This wiki covers setting up your mod folder, finding game objects, hooking functions and troubleshooting scripts. Start with a small change, check it in-game, and build from there. You don’t need Unreal Editor for the Lua examples here. UE4SS loads your scripts alongside the game and lets them interact with objects and functions that Unreal exposes at runtime. Before you start The examples come from the Windows demo. Game updates can change class names, functions and file layouts. Keep a backup of your saves and check your own build’s log when something behaves differently. Getting started If you’re new to Unreal modding, follow these pages in order. Guide What you’ll do Installing UE4SS Set up the loader and check that it starts. Your first mod Create a Lua script and find its output in the log. Working with hooks Run code when a game function is called. Debugging Track down missing objects, Lua errors and crashes. What can you do with Lua? Lua mods can inspect loaded objects, call reflected functions, respond to game events and create UI using assets the game already contains. Some changes need only a few lines of code. Adjust workshop behaviour and add controls to existing menus. Read weapon or target information for tools and overlays. Spawn and arrange existing assets at runtime. Add keybinds, diagnostics and small quality-of-life changes. These are starting points, not a guarantee that every game system is accessible. See Custom content for the limits of a Lua-only mod. A small example A mod begins with Scripts/main.lua . This is enough to check that the loader is running your file: print(\"[MyFirstMod] Hello from Ballistic!\\n\") The message appears in ue4ss/UE4SS.log after you launch the game. The first mod guide shows the complete folder structure. Using this wiki Use the sidebar to browse, or press Ctrl + K to search the text of every guide. Each article has links to its sections and a copy button beside code examples. For the complete loader API, use the UE4SS documentation . The game-specific paths here are observations from the demo, rather than a supported game SDK. All guides Getting started Introduction Installing UE4SS Your first mod Lua scripting Working with hooks Finding game objects Timers & cleanup Reading data tables Debugging Weapons Weapon assemblies Attachments & rails Weapon stats Shots & ballistics Ranges & targets Finding & tracking targets Hits & scoring Range menus & travel Menus & saves Workshop UI Ownership & unlocks Reading & backing up saves Reference Multiplayer architecture Game paths & classes Custom content Packaging a Lua mod Checking game updates Coverage & testing notes Tools & resources"},{"id":"installation","title":"Installing UE4SS","text":"Installing UE4SS Set up the loader, then check the log before adding a mod. What you need The Windows version of Ballistic.exe. A UE4SS release compatible with your game build. A text editor, such as Visual Studio Code. The local demo tests used UE4SS 3.0.1 Beta #0 , commit 5e627997 . This records a tested setup; it is not a recommendation to overwrite a working installation with a different release. Downloads: UE4SS releases · Experimental builds . Find the game folder In Steam, open the game’s settings and choose Manage → Browse local files . From there, open: Ballistic/Binaries/Win64/ You should see Ballistic-Win64-Shipping.exe . The smaller Ballistic.exe in the game’s top-level folder is a launcher. Install the loader beside the shipping executable. Install the loader Close the game. Extract the chosen UE4SS package beside the shipping executable, preserving the package’s folder layout. Merge folders carefully if you already have other mods installed. Launch the game once, then close it and inspect the log. The setup used for these guides has the following layout. Other loader packages can use a different working directory; follow the installation instructions for your release . Win64/ ├── Ballistic-Win64-Shipping.exe ├── dwmapi.dll └── ue4ss/ ├── UE4SS.dll ├── UE4SS-settings.ini ├── UE4SS.log └── Mods/ Check the log Open ue4ss/UE4SS.log . Look for the loader version and startup messages. The file should have a fresh timestamp from your last launch. If it is missing or unchanged, check the extraction location and that the files match your loader release. If the game crashes before mods load, resolve the loader setup before adding scripts. Next step Continue to Your first mod to create a script and verify it with a unique log message."},{"id":"first-mod","title":"Your first mod","text":"Your first mod Create a mod folder, write one Lua file, and check that it runs. Create the folder Inside your loader’s Mods folder, create MyFirstMod . Add a Scripts folder and an empty file named enabled.txt . ue4ss/Mods/ └── MyFirstMod/ ├── enabled.txt └── Scripts/ └── main.lua Enable file extensions in Windows Explorer so the script isn’t accidentally saved as main.lua.txt . Write the script Open main.lua in a text editor and add: local function log(message) print(\"[MyFirstMod] \" .. message .. \"\\n\") end log(\"Loaded successfully.\") Use a distinct prefix in every log message. It makes your output easy to find when several mods are enabled. Run the mod Save the file. Fully close and restart the game. Open ue4ss/UE4SS.log . Search for [MyFirstMod] Loaded successfully. If the message appears, your script is loading. If it doesn’t, check the folder names and the log’s Lua errors. Add a keybind This example logs a message when you press F9. Choose a key that does not conflict with another mod. RegisterKeyBind(Key.F9, function() print(\"[MyFirstMod] F9 pressed.\\n\") end Keep engine work on the game thread A log message is a small first test. If your keybind changes Unreal objects, schedule that work with ExecuteInGameThread . See Finding game objects . Disable or share the mod With the game closed, remove the mod’s enabled.txt to disable it. For a small Lua-only release, package your mod folder, scripts and enable marker with clear installation instructions. Don’t bundle the game, saves, logs or your entire UE4SS installation."},{"id":"hooks","title":"Working with hooks","text":"Working with hooks A hook runs your code when the game calls an Unreal function. Find a function Use the UE4SS object viewer or an object dump to locate the exact reflected function path. Class names and function names are case-sensitive. A Blueprint function often has a path like this: /Game/UI/HUD/Workshop/BunkerHUDItems/Attach_POI.Attach_POI_C:GetCompatibleMountsRecursive This path was observed in the demo’s attachment UI. It may not be loaded until workshop assets are present. Wait for the function Don’t assume a Blueprint is available when main.lua first runs. Check for it on the game thread, and register your hook once. local path = \"/Game/UI/HUD/Workshop/BunkerHUDItems/Attach_POI.Attach_POI_C:GetCompatibleMountsRecursive\" local installed = false LoopAsync(2000, function() if installed then return end ExecuteInGameThread(function() if installed then return end local fn = StaticFindObject(path) if not fn or not fn:IsValid() then return end RegisterHook(path, function(context) local object = context:get() if not object or not object:IsValid() then return end print(\"[MyFirstMod] Workshop lookup called.\\n\") end) installed = true print(\"[MyFirstMod] Hook installed.\\n\") end) end Read parameters carefully Hook arguments can be UE4SS parameter wrappers. Use the access pattern documented for the parameter’s type, and inspect the signature before reading or changing it. Callback phase and return-parameter behaviour can differ between native and Blueprint functions. The sample above only reads the context and logs a call. It does not alter mount compatibility or attachments. Avoid common mistakes Don’t install the same hook on every timer tick. Don’t call the hooked function from its own callback unless you have a deliberate recursion guard. Don’t assume a path from an older game build is still correct. Don’t use a Lua error handler as protection against native memory crashes. Check the UE4SS API documentation for your loader version before writing to parameters or relying on pre/post callbacks."},{"id":"objects","title":"Finding game objects","text":"Finding game objects Find an object, check that it is valid, then use it while it still belongs to the current world. Loaded objects StaticFindObject looks up an object that is already loaded. It does not automatically load every asset from the game archive. ExecuteInGameThread(function() local object = StaticFindObject( \"/Game/Data/Weapons/Attachment_Data.Attachment_Data\" ) if not object or not object:IsValid() then print(\"[MyFirstMod] Attachment table is not loaded yet.\\n\") return end print(\"[MyFirstMod] \" .. object:GetFullName() .. \"\\n\") end Instances and defaults A class describes an object type. A class default object contains its default values. A live actor or widget is an instance. Editing a default is not the same as changing the weapon or menu the player is currently using. Read the full object name and confirm which object you found before writing to it. World changes Travelling from the bunker to a range replaces many actors and widgets. A reference that worked before travel can become invalid afterwards. Reacquire world-specific objects after travel. Check validity immediately before use. Clear cached widget references when the menu is destroyed. Keep track of actors your mod spawns so you can clean them up. Arrays and containers Unreal containers exposed through UE4SS are not always ordinary Lua tables. In this local loader, using ipairs on some Unreal arrays continued beyond the reported length. A bounded loop worked for those arrays: local points = editor.POIs local count = #points assert(count \u003c= 512, \"Unexpected attachment-point count\") for i = 1, count do local point = points[i] if point and point:IsValid() then print(point:GetFullName() .. \"\\n\") end end This example requires a valid editor instance. Set and map containers have different APIs; don’t apply the array pattern to every container. Reflected methods Use methods present in the dump for your build. For actor transforms, the local bindings exposed K2_GetActorLocation and K2_GetActorRotation . Calling an unavailable method produced a TrivialObject error rather than a normal Lua function call."},{"id":"runtime","title":"Timers & cleanup","text":"Timers & cleanup Make a small mod behave properly after menu changes, travel and repeated clicks. Pick a timing source Use a slow discovery loop to wait for assets, a game-thread callback for engine work, and a game clock for elapsed-time calculations. They solve different problems. Task Approach Wait for a widget class A bounded or low-frequency discovery loop. Read or change Unreal objects Run the operation on the game thread. Animate an overlay Use elapsed time; don’t tie speed to callback count. Expire a notification Compare timestamps rather than assuming an exact timer interval. Avoid overlapping work A background loop can queue a new callback before the previous one has run. Keep one pending callback and clear the flag even after a Lua error: local queued = false LoopAsync(1000, function() if queued then return end queued = true ExecuteInGameThread(function() local ok, err = pcall(function() -- Read or update the current world's objects here. end) queued = false if not ok then print(\"[MyMod] \" .. tostring(err) .. \"\\n\") end end) end Cancel stale actions For a queued attachment operation, capture the weapon identity and an operation number when the user clicks. Before every delayed step, check that the same editor is open, the weapon is unchanged and the operation number still matches. Stop when any check fails. Setting a Lua boolean doesn’t cancel a callback already in the queue. The callback needs to check that boolean before doing engine work. Separate world state from settings Keep preferences such as overlay size separate from actor references. On world change, clear actor/widget caches, pending shot IDs and world-specific timers. Rebuild them from the new world. An object name alone can be reused after travel; a world identity plus object identity is a better cache key. Clean up what you create Track only the actors and widgets your mod creates. Remove its widgets from their parents and destroy its actors while they are still valid in that world. Don’t run a global actor sweep and delete everything with a similar class name. If you temporarily change an existing actor’s visibility, collision or physics, remember its original values and restore those values when your feature closes. What was checked Queued-work guards and per-world cleanup are used in the local UI, target and attachment fixtures. This guide is a lifecycle pattern, not a universal timer-cancellation API. The examples describe the local Windows demo setup. See Checking game updates before using them on another build."},{"id":"data-tables","title":"Reading data tables","text":"Reading data tables Find the table, inspect a row, and keep discovery separate from mutation. Find a table The demo has tables for attachment mount definitions and range menu data. You can inspect them once the asset is loaded: ExecuteInGameThread(function() local data = LoadAsset(\"/Game/Data/Ranges/RangeData.RangeData\") if not data or not data:IsValid() then return end local names = data:GetRowNames() for i = 1, #names do print(\"[MyMod] Range row: \" .. tostring(names[i]) .. \"\\n\") end end Read a row In the tested loader, FindRow accepted a Lua string as the row name. Passing an FName in that particular call returned no row. This is different from APIs that explicitly expect an FName . local row = data:FindRow(\"PistolRange\") if row then local map = row.MapName_3_02B8F3864CDF2B67468FDD80A1766519 print(\"[MyMod] Map: \" .. map:ToString() .. \"\\n\") end The long suffix belongs to a Blueprint struct field. Copy the field name from your build’s dump instead of shortening it to MapName and assuming that will work. Useful tables Asset What it describes /Game/Data/Ranges/RangeData.RangeData Range rows, display information and destination names. /Game/Data/Weapons/Attachment_Data.Attachment_Data Attachment mount relationships. The attachment table contained 86 mount rows in the inspected build. That is a build observation, not a constant your mod should require forever. Watch the field types A row can contain names, text, class references and soft class references. A soft reference is not the same thing as a loaded class. Converting, loading or iterating them can trigger additional engine work. Keep an initial probe small: list names, read one known row, then inspect one field. Don’t start by copying the entire table into Lua. Before changing data Changing a row affects every consumer that reads the live table. Rewriting recursive mount relationships can also introduce cycles. A previous broad attachment-row copying experiment froze the game; a targeted lookup hook was used instead. Make any edits reversible, preserve original values and test teardown. Adding a menu row doesn’t create its map or asset. See Range menus & travel . What was checked Row-name listing and string FindRow calls were exercised locally. Broad soft-reference copying is a known failed experiment, not a recommended technique. The examples describe the local Windows demo setup. See Checking game updates before using them on another build."},{"id":"debugging","title":"Debugging","text":"Debugging Start with the log and a repeatable set of steps. Read the log In the layout used here, the loader log is Ballistic/Binaries/Win64/ue4ss/UE4SS.log . Check its timestamp and search for your mod’s prefix. Log when your script loads, when a hook installs and when the relevant callback runs. Those are three different checks. local function log(message) print(\"[MyFirstMod] \" .. tostring(message) .. \"\\n\") end log(\"Script loaded.\") Common errors Symptom What to check No log message Mod folder, enabled.txt, script filename and extraction location. Object is invalid Whether the asset is loaded and whether the world or menu changed. Hook never runs Exact function path, loaded class and whether the game uses that event. TrivialObject is not callable Whether the method is reflected in this game build. Freeze while iterating Container type, loop bounds and recursive hook calls. Native access violation The full crash stack, last callback and recently accessed engine objects. Handle Lua errors A protected call can log a Lua failure and stop that operation: local ok, err = pcall(function() -- Run one operation you are investigating here. end) if not ok then print(\"[MyFirstMod] \" .. tostring(err) .. \"\\n\") end pcall does not make an invalid native pointer safe. If the process crashes inside the engine or loader, simplify the callback and inspect the native stack. Reproduce a problem Write down the game build, loader version and mod version. Use a minimal setup with the mods needed for the test. Record the exact weapon, part or menu involved. Compare the result before and after the operation. Restart fully, then repeat the same steps. For a stat problem, record the value before fitting a part, after fitting it, and after removing it. “It went negative” doesn’t show whether the value dropped below its original baseline. After a game update Check function paths and signatures again. Confirm asset availability, menu construction and save behaviour separately. A successful startup log only proves that startup completed."},{"id":"weapons","title":"Weapon assemblies","text":"Weapon assemblies A visible gun is an assembly of actors and components, not necessarily one mesh. Find the equipped weapon In a range, the local pawn exposes EquippedWeapon . The workshop uses its own displayed weapon reference, so don’t expect the range pawn to identify the gun on the workbench. local H = require(\"UEHelpers\") RegisterKeyBind(Key.F9, function() ExecuteInGameThread(function() local pc = H.GetPlayerController() if not pc or not pc:IsValid() then return end local pawn = pc:K2_GetPawn() if not pawn or not pawn:IsValid() then return end local gun = pawn.EquippedWeapon if not gun or not gun:IsValid() then return end print(\"[MyMod] Equipped: \" .. gun:GetFullName() .. \"\\n\") end) end Parts and components Observed weapon classes include C_WeaponBase , C_WeaponPart and the Blueprint A_WeaponBase_C . Parts expose a ParentWeapon reference. ChildActorComponents can own attached part actors, which can have their own descendants. Enumerate the assembly before changing it. A core barrel, a fitted rail and a nested foregrip can live at different depths. Reading just the root actor’s mesh misses much of the weapon. local children = {} gun:GetAllChildActors(children, true) for i = 1, math.min(#children, 128) do local child = children[i] if child and child:IsValid() then print(\"[MyMod] Part: \" .. child:GetFullName() .. \"\\n\") end end Templates are different from live actors CurrentWeaponTemplate records a selected assembly. A spawned weapon is the live actor built from it. Changing the visible actor alone does not establish that the saved template changed, and changing a template does not automatically refresh every existing actor. The observed template has separate weapon-class, main-part, attachment and grip data. The generated field names have suffixes; inspect the current struct rather than copying offsets. Visible doesn’t mean usable A mesh proxy can show another player’s gun without implementing its firing mechanism. A usable gun also needs the correct actor class, parts, ammunition, ownership and input path. Test local use separately from remote appearance. Likewise, hiding a source gun and drawing a replacement mesh does not make a new inventory weapon. Test an assembly Record the selected template and visible part tree. Change one part and confirm only the expected branch changes. Leave the workbench and test handling and firing. Travel to a range and back. Restart and check the saved assembly separately. What was checked EquippedWeapon lookup and assembled mesh capture were used in local range fixtures. Arbitrary replacement weapons, imported models and every possible assembly are not verified. The examples describe the local Windows demo setup. See Checking game updates before using them on another build."},{"id":"attachments","title":"Attachments & rails","text":"Attachments & rails Separate the attachment list, the mounting point and the part fitted to it. The attachment flow The workbench creates Attach_POI_C widgets for attachment points. A point references its AttachPoint component, the current weapon and a popup list. Compatibility lookup decides which attachment classes that list can offer. Entry point Observed role GetCompatibleMountsRecursive Resolve compatible mount definitions. OnGetUserMenuContent Build the attachment popup. SetupAttachment Apply a class or remove a fitted part. AttachPoint.ChildActor The actor currently fitted at that point. Nested rails A rail can add mounting points of its own. A grip fitted to the rail is a descendant of that rail, not necessarily a direct child of the base weapon. Removing a parent can invalidate the child actor and its UI at the same time. When iterating points during a clear operation, reacquire the current point list after each change. Don’t keep using an old list after the workbench rebuilds it. Fit and remove through the game For a valid live attachment-point widget, the native action takes a class reference to fit a part. Passing nil removed it in local tests: -- Context: poi is a valid live Attach_POI widget. -- This changes the assembly; it is not an inspection call. poi:SetupAttachment(nil) Let the game handle default-part behaviour and its refresh chain. Directly destroying a child actor bypasses that UI action and may leave the template or derived stats out of date. Compatibility isn’t ownership A part can be mount-compatible but not bought. A shop entry can be visible but locked. A saved assembly may contain a part that a purchased-set-only check misses. Keep these questions separate; see Ownership & unlocks . Test repeated edits Record stats before fitting, after fitting and after removal. Repeat with the same rail, then try removing the rail while a child part is still mounted. Check that a cancelled operation doesn’t remove a part from a newly selected gun. For a stat report, keep the exact weapon, rail and child class names. A vague “negative stat” report is not enough to decide whether an extra subtraction occurred. What was checked Single native removals, synthetic three-point clear batches and rebuilding/cancellation guards passed local fixtures. These do not prove every real fitted assembly saves and reloads correctly. The examples describe the local Windows demo setup. See Checking game updates before using them on another build."},{"id":"weapon-stats","title":"Weapon stats","text":"Weapon stats Measure a baseline before trying to fix a stat that looks wrong. Part values and weapon totals Field Where it was observed ErgoModifier Per-part ergonomics modifier on C_WeaponPart. TotalErgo Weapon’s current ergonomics total. TotalInstalledErgo Separate installed-part ergonomics total. TotalRecoilControlModifier Weapon recoil-control aggregate. ParentWeapon Part’s associated weapon reference. Current and installed ergonomics are different fields. Don’t substitute one for the other in an overlay. A part may also affect recoil, weight, cooling or grip behaviour rather than just ergonomics. Read a baseline -- Context: gun is a valid live weapon, on the game thread. print(string.format( \"[MyMod] current=%.6f installed=%.6f recoil=%.6f\\n\", gun.TotalErgo, gun.TotalInstalledErgo, gun.TotalRecoilControlModifier )) Recalculation The Blueprint weapon base has UpdateCharacteristicsFromParts . Its nine boolean inputs in the inspected build are: Weight, Ergo, CenterMassLoc, Recoil, GripCenter, BarrelLength, EquippedScopeAndLaser, Cooling, TotalLength This is a mutating recalculation function. Confirm the signature in your own dump before calling it. Prefer observing the normal attachment action first, rather than forcing a recalculation on every frame and masking a broken refresh chain. A removal test A local Sten fixture used a barrel strap and nested ergonomic broom grip. The grip’s modifier was approximately +0.15 . Ten fit/remove cycles with the expanded compatibility hook active gave: Step Installed ergo Rail-only baseline −0.600000 Grip fitted −0.450000 Grip removed −0.600000 That fixture started negative and returned to the same negative baseline. It did not show a cumulative penalty. It was a synthetic assembly, so those numbers are not the correct expected totals for every player’s Sten. Report a stat bug Include weapon/rail/attachment names, all three snapshots, game and loader versions, and whether the result survives leaving the workbench or restarting. Compare ordinary and expanded compatibility where possible. Don’t clamp every negative number to zero. That can hide legitimate penalties and conceal the actual bug. What was checked The ten-cycle fixture ran on 6 November 2026 and returned to baseline without manual refresh between cycles. The reported real-player rail-removal penalty remains unconfirmed. The examples describe the local Windows demo setup. See Checking game updates before using them on another build."},{"id":"ballistics","title":"Shots & ballistics","text":"Shots & ballistics A fired shot, a projectile impact and a valid target hit are separate events. The shot pipeline Event What it tells you A_WeaponBase_C:ProcessBulletFired Weapon firing event with cartridge, barrel and created bullet information. C_BulletBase:RegisterHit A bullet reported impact information. TargetBase_C:Hit Target hit path with projectile attribution in the inspected signature. TargetBase_C:OnHit Target reports validity, energy, velocity and bonus-related flags. A projectile can strike non-target geometry. A target callback can be invalid. A shot counter and a scoring system should not increment from the same undifferentiated “hit” event. Observe firing Once the Blueprint function is loaded, a read-only hook can establish when it runs: local path = \"/Game/BlueprintLogic/Weapons/A_WeaponBase.A_WeaponBase_C:ProcessBulletFired\" -- Register once, after StaticFindObject(path) is valid. RegisterHook(path, function(context) local gun = context:get() if gun and gun:IsValid() then print(\"[MyMod] Fired: \" .. gun:GetFullName() .. \"\\n\") end end) Observed shot arguments After the context, the inspected firing signature has Cartridge , bullet and gas recoil forces, recoil location/direction, Barrel , powder amount, exit pressure, powder type and a Bullet array. Read the exact signature and unwrap parameters according to their type before using them. Do not assume one event always produces exactly one bullet actor. Keep a shot ID and map each resulting projectile to it. Impacts, energy and units The target’s OnHit signature contains HitEnergy and HitVelocity , but a field name alone does not establish the units or all conversions used by the game. Record the raw values and compare controlled shots before labelling them in an overlay. Don’t replace the game’s trajectory with a camera ray and call it the actual bullet path. A straight line can be useful as a cosmetic tracer, but it does not reproduce drop, penetration or ricochets. What is not documented here This guide maps events, not the complete internal ballistics model. Cartridge construction, powder curves, drag coefficients, penetration materials and every ammunition unlock rule still need dedicated research. For score handling, continue to Hits & scoring . What was checked Firing and impact hooks resolved in local range checks; native target hit callbacks were also exercised. No full trajectory or ammunition-physics replacement has been validated. The examples describe the local Windows demo setup. See Checking game updates before using them on another build."},{"id":"targets","title":"Finding & tracking targets","text":"Finding & tracking targets Find the range’s target actors, then inspect the component that actually moves. Discover targets The inspected range uses TargetBase_C and derived classes. Get the active world from the viewport, then query that world: local H = require(\"UEHelpers\") ExecuteInGameThread(function() local viewport = H.GetGameViewportClient() if not viewport or not viewport:IsValid() then return end local world = viewport:GetWorld() if not world or not world:IsValid() then return end local class = LoadAsset( \"/Game/BlueprintLogic/Targets/TargetBase.TargetBase_C\" ) if not class or not class:IsValid() then return end local gameplay = StaticFindObject(\"/Script/Engine.Default__GameplayStatics\") local targets = {} gameplay:GetAllActorsOfClass(world, class, targets) print(\"[MyMod] Targets found: \" .. #targets .. \"\\n\") end Useful fields Field Observed use HasBeenHit Whether the target has been marked hit. ValidForOneHitOnly Reject further scoring hits after the first. HitValue Current target value. MinHitValue Lower bound used when reducing a value. HitValueMultOnHit Multiplier used by the local hit-state implementation. isMoving Hint used to prioritise moving target samples. Actor versus component motion A physics mesh can move while its owning actor’s transform stays unchanged. Capturing only K2_GetActorLocation can therefore show a target standing upright remotely while its mesh has fallen over locally. Inspect the primitive, static-mesh or skeletal-mesh component that carries the visible body. Capture its world transform separately. Different target classes can need different component selection. Sampling and caching Discover actors infrequently and sample known targets on a bounded budget. Give moving and recently hit targets priority. A local registry once contained hundreds of targets; rediscovering or loading assets for every target on every frame is unnecessary. Invalidate the registry after travel. Runtime addresses identify local objects only; they are not stable network IDs. Resetting targets Clearing HasBeenHit alone does not restore a broken target, a displaced physics body, its collision, or a reduced score value. A complete reset needs the original state of each relevant field and component. Restarting the range can be a more reliable first reset implementation. What was checked Local discovery, target baselines, distinct synthetic targets and actor/component motion were tested. The documented registry strategy is a mod implementation, not a built-in replication service. The examples describe the local Windows demo setup. See Checking game updates before using them on another build."},{"id":"scoring","title":"Hits & scoring","text":"Hits & scoring Count one valid gameplay event once, and keep your own score separate from progression. Target callbacks The observed OnHit signature passes ValidHit , player, impact information, hit energy, hit velocity, bonus flag, cartridge-unlock flag and breaking-hit flag. Character targets have their own matching hook path in the inspected build. Inspect validity before scoring. Registering both base and derived handlers without attribution or deduplication can count a single physical shot more than once. Attribute a hit Give each local firing event a monotonically increasing shot ID. Associate created bullet actors with that shot. Use the target’s hit path to connect the bullet and target. Check valid-hit state before adding a score. Retire old shot and bullet references after a short bounded window. Do not attribute every hit to “the most recent shot” when the weapon can have several projectiles in flight. Two players firing at once makes that mistake especially visible. Deduplicate events For a custom per-target hit score, one useful key is round + player + shot + target . Decide explicitly whether pellets or repeated contacts should score independently for your mode. Deduplication policy is game design, not just a networking detail. -- Custom mode state, not the game's persistent score. local seen = {} local score = 0 local function award(roundId, playerId, shotId, targetId, points) local key = table.concat({roundId, playerId, shotId, targetId}, \":\") if seen[key] then return false end if type(points) ~= \"number\" or points ~= points then return false end if points \u003c 0 or points > 100000 then return false end seen[key] = true score = score + math.floor(points) return true end Cosmetics are not rewards A replicated impact can play a reaction without replaying the full local scoring/reward event. The local target fixture used CosmeticOnHit for visual response, with custom score handled separately. That does not make the function a complete substitute for the game’s normal hit logic. Do not replay the ordinary hit event merely to display a remote impact: it may grant progression, unlock ammunition or change target value again. Round timing Use explicit countdown, playing, settling and finished phases. For network play, define whether a hit near the deadline is accepted by host receipt time, synchronised event time or a bounded grace policy. Cancel or pause when the connection changes instead of letting an invisible round keep running. What was checked Local fixtures exercised valid native hits, one-hit rejection, cosmetic reactions and separate session scores. Internet-delay fairness and simultaneous real-peer shooting are separate tests, not proven by a local fixture. The examples describe the local Windows demo setup. See Checking game updates before using them on another build."},{"id":"ranges","title":"Range menus & travel","text":"Range menus & travel A range card is UI data. A playable destination also needs a cooked map and a working travel path. Range menu data The range table is /Game/Data/Ranges/RangeData.RangeData . The PistolRange row mapped to Range_SwimmingPool in local checks. Its display label is Azure Swimming Pool. The inspected table also had rows such as Valley and ShoppingMall . Their presence did not prove those maps were usable in the demo. What makes a card appear? Two observed UI entry points are RangeList.PreConstruct and RangeListItem.SetSelected . The menu also checks the game instance’s UnlockedRanges , so adding a row alone may leave the card absent. A runtime-only custom card was made visible locally by adding its row name to the in-memory unlocked list. That was an experimental menu change, not a demonstrated permanent unlock or newly cooked map. Confirm the destination Level Package / result Bunker_Main /Game/Maps/Bunker/Bunker_Main Range_SwimmingPool /Game/Maps/Ranges/Range_SwimmingPool Range_Valley A custom-card travel attempt returned to the main menu; not a working demo destination. Use a small explicit allowlist of destinations you tested. Don’t assemble arbitrary map paths from a user-entered label or assume every table row exists in the archive. Local and shared travel Opening a map for one local player is different from moving a connected listen-server session. The local multiplayer code used the AdvancedSessions ServerTravel path with bUseSeamlessTravel enabled and a ?listen destination. That solution depends on the session and driver already being valid. Appending ?listen to an arbitrary OpenLevel call is not a complete multiplayer implementation. After arrival Confirm the actual current level name and world identity. Reacquire controllers, pawns, weapons, targets and UI. Confirm the local player has a usable weapon and ammunition. For multiplayer, confirm both peers arrived before starting a round. Try restart, return to bunker and repeat travel. Building a detached scene by teleporting within the pool world still uses that world’s lighting and lifecycle. See Custom content . What was checked Pool travel, restart and return were exercised in local native/listen fixtures. Two-PC travel reliability and absent demo maps are not implied by those checks. The examples describe the local Windows demo setup. See Checking game updates before using them on another build."},{"id":"workshop-ui","title":"Workshop UI","text":"Workshop UI Reuse the game’s widgets, but create your own controls and callbacks. Find the workshop The observed HUD class is /Game/UI/HUD/Workshop/HUD_Workshop.HUD_Workshop_C . Its attachment editor is WeaponEditMOde , with that exact spelling. The HUD and attachment editor are related but have different lifetimes. A button placed on the HUD canvas needs its visibility tied to entering and leaving attachment edit mode. Create a native button The common TheRangeButton_C class provides the game’s existing button appearance. This creates a separate widget; it does not click or duplicate Save and exit: local H = require(\"UEHelpers\") ExecuteInGameThread(function() local pc = H.GetPlayerController() if not pc or not pc:IsValid() then return end local class = LoadAsset(\"/Game/UI/Common/TheRangeButton.TheRangeButton_C\") if not class or not class:IsValid() then return end local library = StaticFindObject(\"/Script/UMG.Default__WidgetBlueprintLibrary\") local button = library:Create(pc, class, pc) if not button or not button:IsValid() then return end button.Text = FText(\"My action\") button.NavText:SetText(FText(\"My action\")) -- Add button to your valid parent panel and retain a reference. end Place the control For a CanvasPanel parent, add with AddChildToCanvas and set the resulting CanvasPanelSlot’s anchors, alignment, position and size. Position numbers are relative to the anchors, not always screen pixels from the top left. A local workbench check found Save and exit at X=50, Y=-50 , size 200×40 , with bottom-left anchors and bottom alignment. A button directly above it used Y=-100 , leaving a ten-unit gap. Those numbers describe that HUD layout, not every resolution or future build. Don’t copy action delegates Copy appearance properties deliberately, not the original widget’s action bindings. A copied Save and exit delegate can execute the original action when your new button is pressed. One local implementation handled the left-mouse keybind and checked the native button’s hover state. That verified a mouse path only; it does not establish controller, keyboard, touch or accessibility support. Prefer a verified button event route when your loader and widget expose one. Rebuilds and input Prevent duplicate construction by checking that your widget still has a valid parent. Hide it outside edit mode, cancel pending actions when the displayed weapon changes, and restore any input mode or cursor state you changed. Test hover, click, closing the menu and opening it again. A widget object existing in memory does not prove that it is visible or clickable. What was checked Native button construction, matching canvas layout, duplicate prevention and edit-mode visibility passed local fixtures. Full interaction on every fitted player weapon and input device was not established. The examples describe the local Windows demo setup. See Checking game updates before using them on another build."},{"id":"progression","title":"Ownership & unlocks","text":"Ownership & unlocks “Visible”, “unlocked”, “purchased” and “fitted” are different states. The relevant containers Game-instance field Observed representation PurchasedParts Set of soft class references for purchased parts. UnlockedWeapons Array of soft weapon-class references. UnlockedRanges Array of range row names. SavedWeaponTemplates Map containing saved weapon assemblies. A template can contain part paths that aren’t matched by a purchased-set-only check. That does not prove those paths all represent independently owned, currently available attachments. Why simple filters fail Three local attachment-menu experiments illustrate the problem: A button-enabled check let every entry through, so enabled state did not express ownership. PurchasedParts alone matched far fewer menu entries than the player expected. A union of purchased and saved-template paths still missed expected choices in the live menu. Those filters were not a completed ownership API. They were removed from the stable expanded catalog. Match reference types Passing a loaded UClass directly to a soft-reference set caused a native crash in an earlier filter. An isolated class-to-soft-reference conversion probe was safe for one unrelated class, but that did not validate every menu membership check. Inspect the stored type, the API’s expected type and the exact path form. Package paths, object paths and generated Blueprint class paths are related but not identical strings. Design an owned-only menu Define whether “owned” includes default, bought and currently fitted parts. Start with a small set of attachments whose ownership the player can confirm. Compare those parts with the game’s normal menu. Use stable identifiers and explain each exclusion in a diagnostic log. Test pagination after filtering, including an empty result and a single page. Do not present “we found many save paths” as proof the filter is correct. The list the player can actually select is the test that matters. Unlocking content Adding an unlocked row name in memory can make a menu card appear. It doesn’t establish permanent progression, the presence of the associated map, or correct save persistence. A production unlock mod needs those checks separately. What was checked The container types and failed filtering outcomes are recorded local observations. A complete reliable owned-only attachment filter remains unresolved. The examples describe the local Windows demo setup. See Checking game updates before using them on another build."},{"id":"saves","title":"Reading & backing up saves","text":"Reading & backing up saves Start read-only. A live game instance and a save file are not the same snapshot. Save location The local demo uses %LOCALAPPDATA%/Ballistic/Saved/SaveGames/BallisticDemo.sav . Close the game and copy the save folder somewhere separate before an experiment that can change progression or assemblies. Keep backups out of your mod release and out of a public repository. A useful backup is a dated copy you can identify and restore, not a file overwritten on every test. Load without applying GameplayStatics can load the persisted slot into a save object. Loading it for inspection is different from replacing the game instance’s active state: local H = require(\"UEHelpers\") ExecuteInGameThread(function() local viewport = H.GetGameViewportClient() if not viewport or not viewport:IsValid() then return end local world = viewport:GetWorld() if not world or not world:IsValid() then return end local gameplay = StaticFindObject(\"/Script/Engine.Default__GameplayStatics\") local instance = gameplay:GetGameInstance(world) if not instance or not instance:IsValid() then return end local save = gameplay:LoadGameFromSlot(\"BallisticDemo\", instance.UserIndex) if save and save:IsValid() then print(\"[MyMod] Save class: \" .. save:GetClass():GetFullName() .. \"\\n\") end end Known save class /Game/BlueprintLogic/Core/Game/Save/BallisticSaveGame.BallisticSaveGame_C Do not assume that copying properties from one object to another is a complete restore. The local save-service code also refreshes game-instance data and invalidates selected loadouts and cached range lists after applying a snapshot. Snapshot versus live state A file copy contains the last persisted state. Unsaved workbench edits or in-memory progression can differ. “Host save to guest” is a one-way snapshot transfer unless you explicitly implement a merge or ongoing replication. A sharing feature should distinguish accepting a download from applying it. Bind the final confirmation to the exact peer and transfer, reject incompatible data, back up the guest’s original file, and handle failed loads with a recovery path. Verify a save feature Use disposable profiles for write tests. Record a file hash before a read-only probe and compare it afterwards. For a restore feature, test a controlled backup/apply/restore cycle and then restart the game. A success message from a copy operation does not prove the game can load the result. What was checked Read-only slot loading and native export checks ran locally. Local save-sharing code exists, but real two-PC guest application was not verified in the recorded test pass. The examples describe the local Windows demo setup. See Checking game updates before using them on another build."},{"id":"multiplayer","title":"Multiplayer architecture","text":"Multiplayer architecture Connecting two players is the beginning. Shared game state still needs explicit rules. Connection isn’t replication A Steam session or invitation identifies and connects peers. It does not automatically make every single-player actor, UI action, weapon part or score replicate. The local multiplayer implementation combines a listen-server travel path with a native Steam bridge and custom state messages. That architecture is a mod implementation, not an official multiplayer API supplied by this wiki. Separate state from events Send as state Send as events Player pose, aim and equipped appearance A shot, a hit or a chat message. Target pose and current hit state A round-start or recovery request. Current round phase and scores A one-time save offer or acceptance. Snapshots can supersede older snapshots. One-time events need IDs, acknowledgement and duplicate handling. Replaying the same acknowledged hit must not award its points again. Authority and validation Choose who decides travel, target state and scoring. A host-authoritative mode should validate the sender, session, round, target identity and bounded event values before applying a guest request. Never accept arbitrary asset paths, file paths or Lua source from a network message. Use explicit allowlists and fixed save slots where appropriate. Movement and visible guns Render remote movement from buffered snapshots rather than snapping directly to each packet. Reset the buffer for teleport or world changes. Interpolation hides normal packet gaps; it does not fix wrong authority or missing state. Remote weapon appearance can be reconstructed from mesh references and relative transforms. Reuse unchanged meshes and clean up replaced ones. A cosmetic proxy is not another fully simulated gun actor. Interruptions and testing Give every session and world generation a distinct identity. Discard stale packets after reconnect or travel. Cancel active rounds when a player disconnects, and recover through an explicit ready/start flow. Test on two PCs/accounts: simultaneous shots, the same target hit by both peers, gun switching, range restart, repeated travel and a disconnect near round end. A local simulated-peer test cannot establish real network behaviour under delay. What was checked Local transport fixtures, actor rendering and native gameplay checks exist; the user previously confirmed basic two-PC connection. Later simultaneous shooting, save application and delay-fairness scenarios were not verified in that recorded pass. The examples describe the local Windows demo setup. See Checking game updates before using them on another build."},{"id":"paths","title":"Game paths & classes","text":"Game paths & classes Useful locations and names observed in the Windows demo. Build-specific reference These paths were checked locally on Steam demo build 26083142 . They are a starting point for discovery, not a promise that another release has the same objects. File locations Location Purpose Ballistic/Binaries/Win64/ Shipping executable and loader entry point. Win64/ue4ss/Mods/ Mod folders in the tested loader layout. Win64/ue4ss/UE4SS.log Loader and Lua output; paths here start at Binaries. %LOCALAPPDATA%/Ballistic/Saved/SaveGames/ Player saves. Keep these out of mod releases. Ballistic/Content/Paks/ Cooked game content archives. Workshop functions The attachment point widget uses this class path: /Game/UI/HUD/Workshop/BunkerHUDItems/Attach_POI.Attach_POI_C Function Observed role GetCompatibleMountsRecursive Mount-compatibility lookup. OnGetUserMenuContent Attachment popup creation. SetupAttachment Fitting or removing an attachment. The workshop HUD is /Game/UI/HUD/Workshop/HUD_Workshop.HUD_Workshop_C . Its attachment editor is named WeaponEditMOde ; preserve that spelling when looking it up. Weapon classes /Game/BlueprintLogic/Weapons/A_WeaponBase.A_WeaponBase_C /Script/Ballistic.C_WeaponBase /Script/Ballistic.C_WeaponPart The Blueprint weapon base exposes UpdateCharacteristicsFromParts . In the inspected build it has nine boolean inputs. Check the full signature in your own dump before calling it. Maps Name Observed use Bunker_Main Bunker and workshop. Range_SwimmingPool Azure Swimming Pool range. The confirmed pool package is /Game/Maps/Ranges/Range_SwimmingPool . A range row or menu label alone does not prove that its associated map is included in the demo. For the behaviour behind these names, see Weapon assemblies , Attachments & rails , Shots & ballistics and Range menus & travel . Check your build Use the loader’s object viewer or dump to confirm paths. Keep a small text record of the build, object name, signature and action you tested. See Debugging for a repeatable check."},{"id":"custom-content","title":"Custom content","text":"Custom content Know which changes can reuse game assets and which need an asset pipeline. Lua-only mods Lua is useful for changing behaviour, creating UI and assembling objects from assets already cooked into the game. You can prototype layouts by spawning existing meshes and components in the current world. That does not create a new cooked map. The scene still belongs to the existing world, with its lighting, streaming and lifecycle. New models and textures An external .fbx file is a source model, not a game-ready Unreal asset. A Lua script cannot simply use an FBX path as a normal skeletal mesh reference. Proper mesh, material and animation integration generally needs compatible cooked assets or a dedicated runtime importer. This wiki does not provide an importer. Opening the game in Unreal The installed game contains cooked content, rather than the developer’s editable source project. Installing Unreal Editor does not restore the original Blueprints, levels or source assets. The local executable reported the UE5.7 release branch. That is useful context when investigating tools, but it does not establish that any specific editor patch will produce compatible content. Testing a runtime scene Check mesh and material availability in the actual game build. Test lighting and exposure in the world where you spawn objects. Verify collision and a safe player spawn. Keep references to the actors your mod owns. Remove them when the scene closes or the world changes. For a runtime prototype, start with Range menus & travel and Timers & cleanup . For distributing an asset-free Lua mod, see Packaging a Lua mod . Further reading Dmgvol’s UE modding guides cover several Unreal modding approaches. Start with Tools & resources for the loader and archive-inspection references used here."},{"id":"packaging","title":"Packaging a Lua mod","text":"Packaging a Lua mod Make the archive easy to install and leave your development setup out of it. Pick an archive root For an archive intended to be extracted into ue4ss/Mods , include the mod folder itself: MyMod/ ├── enabled.txt └── Scripts/ ├── main.lua ├── config.lua └── feature.lua README.md Another valid approach is to include the full Ballistic/Binaries/Win64/ue4ss/Mods/MyMod path and instruct the user to extract into the game folder. Choose one root and describe it exactly. Mixing the instructions produces doubled or missing folders. Include only release files Use an explicit file list in the build script instead of zipping the whole project. Include the Lua files the mod loads, its enable marker, required mod-owned binaries and the player instructions. Exclude saves, backup saves, captured screenshots, object dumps, runtime reports, logs, tests, browser profiles and unrelated mods. Don’t package the game or the entire loader with a small Lua mod. Version the whole mod Put a version in the startup log and give the archive the same version. A native bridge and its Lua scripts can depend on each other, so ship a complete matching release rather than telling users to update just one file. If a protocol changes, check the peer’s protocol version before accepting gameplay messages. An application version label alone does not prove wire compatibility. Write installation instructions Name the loader requirement and tested game build. Show the exact extraction destination and final folder layout. List controls and configuration file locations. Explain the full-restart requirement used by your build. Explain how to disable the mod and what persisted changes remain. Removing a compatibility script does not necessarily undo an assembly already saved by the game. Check the archive Inspect the file list and compare packaged files with the ones you tested. Try extracting into an empty staging folder and reading the instructions as a new user. For an updated release, confirm that renamed or removed modules cannot remain active from the old install. A checksum can identify the exact artifact you tested; it is not a substitute for testing the artifact’s behaviour."},{"id":"compatibility","title":"Checking game updates","text":"Checking game updates Treat an update as a fresh compatibility check, not an automatic reason to change code. Record the build Write down the Steam build ID, executable version, UE4SS version/commit and your mod version. Keep a copy of the previous release so you can distinguish a new game change from a changed script. The local demo records used build 26083142 and a UE5.7 release-branch executable. These are recorded reference points, not a claim that the reader’s installed game is that build. Check the integration surface Area Check Hooks Function exists, class is loaded, signature and callback parameters still match. Properties Field type and generated name still match; don’t reuse an old offset. Assets Expected class, mesh, material and sound actually load. UI Construction, input, layout, close/reopen and duplicate prevention. Gameplay Equip, fire, hit, switch weapon, restart and travel. Saves Read-only checks first; controlled write/restore tests on a disposable profile. Test in stages Launch the loader without your feature enabled. Enable the mod and confirm clean startup. Trigger one relevant callback and inspect its arguments. Use the actual menu or gameplay feature. Repeat operations that rebuild objects or travel worlds. Run separate multi-PC tests for a multiplayer feature. Stop changing code once a check passes unless there is a new failure or unresolved concern. A large refactor during a compatibility audit makes the result harder to interpret. Record what passed Keep test descriptions specific: “registered eight hooks” is different from “both peers fired and hit the same target”. Record the test method and its limits along with the result. The recorded October demo update passed local menu, travel, model and synthetic gameplay checks. The real two-PC scenarios still had a narrower verified scope. Those two facts can coexist. Report a regression Include reproduction steps, exact content names, version details, a fresh relevant log excerpt and what changed from the last working build. Remove personal paths and IDs before posting logs publicly. If you cannot reproduce a reported issue, preserve the report and the tested limits. Don’t mark it fixed because an unrelated synthetic case passed."},{"id":"coverage","title":"Coverage & testing notes","text":"Coverage & testing notes What the wiki explains, what was exercised, and what still needs research. How to read coverage The README percentage is a rough estimate of game-specific documentation, not a measured fraction of all source code, assets or possible mods. Adding setup pages does not establish that more gameplay systems are fully understood. The useful measure is whether a guide gives you a reliable entry point, an example and a clear limit. The table below makes those limits visible without treating every topic as complete. System coverage Area Covered here Still missing Lua setup & lifecycle Loader layout, hooks, discovery, threading, cleanup and debugging. Every loader version and complete API reference. Weapons & attachments Assembly references, rail nesting, native removal and selected stat fields. Every weapon, assembly rule and save/reload combination. Shots & ammunition Shot, projectile-impact and target callback entry points. Complete cartridge construction and ballistic equations. Targets & scoring Discovery, component motion, valid hits, deduplication and custom round design. All target types, rewards and full progression rules. Workshop UI Native button construction, canvas layout and edit-mode lifecycle. Every menu, controller navigation and all accessibility paths. Ranges Table rows, unlock checks, pool travel and restart workflow. A custom cooked-map pipeline and all full-game destinations. Saves & ownership Known slot/class, read-only loading and container distinctions. Full save schema, reliable owned-only filter and all migration cases. Multiplayer State/event separation, authority, visual proxies and interruption handling. A general-purpose SDK and completed real-peer testing of every feature. Evidence levels Found in a dump: the name or field existed in the inspected build. Native fixture: an operation ran against actual engine objects in an isolated test. Local gameplay check: the feature was exercised in the game’s menu or range. Real peer check: separate PCs/accounts performed the scenario together. These levels are different, not interchangeable. A native fixture does not automatically prove a player’s saved assembly or an internet session works. Known open questions The exact owned-only attachment rule remains unresolved. A reported rail-removal stat penalty was not reproduced by the recorded synthetic fixture. A visible custom range card did not establish a new map, and runtime-scene lighting experiments did not produce a finished custom range. The ballistics , ownership and multiplayer guides explain the relevant boundaries in more detail. Add a finding Record the game build, loader version, exact object/function and steps you tested. A small reproducible result is more useful than a long list of untested classes. Follow the repository’s CONTRIBUTING.md when opening a documentation PR."},{"id":"tools","title":"Tools & resources","text":"Tools & resources The main references to keep open while you work. UE4SS The loader runs Lua mods and exposes Unreal objects and functions. Match API guidance to your installed version; the development documentation may describe APIs newer than your local loader. Releases and downloads Installation guide Development documentation Lua API overview Game inspection Start with the UE4SS object viewer and object dump to inspect what the current game session has loaded. Use an archive browser when you need to look for cooked content that isn’t loaded yet. FModel — Unreal archive browser. FModel getting started — configuration and usage. Tool support depends on the game’s engine and archive format. Finding an asset in an archive does not guarantee it can be loaded in every game mode. Unreal modding UE_Modding by Dmgvol collects techniques and guides for Unreal games. Use it to research asset-based mods alongside the Lua approach described here. The game Ballistic.exe on Steam This wiki documents local observations and practical examples. It does not ship a game framework, SDK or game assets."}];
